1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,857
Go Part 22, pointer

2
00:00:01,857 --> 00:00:03,802
versus value receivers.

3
00:00:04,022 --> 00:00:05,298
These two methods have

4
00:00:05,298 --> 00:00:06,458
the exact same body.

5
00:00:06,678 --> 00:00:07,870
So why does only one

6
00:00:07,870 --> 00:00:09,061
of them really work?

7
00:00:09,781 --> 00:00:11,318
We call IncValue, and

8
00:00:11,318 --> 00:00:12,490
N is still zero.

9
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:14,322
Then we call IncPointer,

10
00:00:14,322 --> 00:00:15,532
and N becomes one.

11
00:00:16,252 --> 00:00:17,702
A value receiver gets a

12
00:00:17,702 --> 00:00:18,900
copy of the struct.

13
00:00:19,120 --> 00:00:20,518
The method adds one to

14
00:00:20,518 --> 00:00:21,916
the copy, and the copy

15
00:00:21,916 --> 00:00:22,869
is thrown away.

16
00:00:23,089 --> 00:00:24,690
Your c never changes.

17
00:00:25,410 --> 00:00:26,727
A pointer receiver gets

18
00:00:26,727 --> 00:00:27,701
the address of c.

19
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:28,752
So it changes

20
00:00:28,752 --> 00:00:29,839
the real counter.

21
00:00:30,059 --> 00:00:31,753
Go even takes the address

22
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:33,243
for you when you write

23
00:00:33,243 --> 00:00:34,395
c dot IncPointer.

24
00:00:35,115 --> 00:00:37,216
Recap: a value receiver works

25
00:00:37,216 --> 00:00:38,665
on a copy, a pointer

26
00:00:38,665 --> 00:00:40,838
receiver changes the original.

